      "source": [
        "---\n",
        "title: \"Are EU regions converging?\"\n",
        "subtitle: \"Project C: income and employment gaps between EU regions, 2000-2024\"\n",
        "author: \"Project C Group 7\"\n",
        "format:\n",
        "  html:\n",
        "    toc: true\n",
        "    embed-resources: true\n",
        "    code-fold: true\n",
        "execute:\n",
        "  warning: false\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "## Our question\n",
        "\n",
        "> Between 2000 and 2024, did EU27 NUTS2 regions converge in GDP per head (PPS) and employment rate (ages 20-64), and which groups of regions did not follow the pattern?\n",
        "\n",
        "## Data\n",
        "\n",
        "### GDP per head\n",
        "\n",
        "GDP per head comes from Eurostat table `nama_10r_2gdp`, in purchasing power standards (PPS, `PPS_EU27_2020_HAB`), for EU27 NUTS2 regions, 2000-2024. Extra-regio territories and non-EU countries were removed, which leaves 244 regions. PPS adjusts for price differences between countries, so it compares what incomes can buy and not only their size in euro. The 2024 values are provisional.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Employment rate\n",
        "\n",
        "Employment rates come from Eurostat table `lfst_r_lfe2emprt`: the share of people who are in work, both sexes, in percent, for EU27 NUTS2 regions, 2000-2024. The main measure covers ages 20-64, the age group of the EU employment target. As a robustness check for Question 2 we repeat the analysis for ages 15-64 (`Y15-64`), taken from the same raw file with the same filters, so the two measures differ only in the age group. Extra-regio territories and non-EU countries were removed, and Slovenian and Dutch regions that were only renamed were joined to their new codes. Regions that were split or merged are not recoded. This is why the employment file has more region codes than the GDP file: the extra codes are old regions (HR04, HU10, IE01, IE02, LT00, PT16-PT18). Question 2 uses the regions that have an employment rate in both 2000 and 2024. Some of these values carry Eurostat flags, mostly for a break in the series or a different definition in 2024. The numbers below come from the cleaned files:"
      ],
      "id": "dade9f82"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| label: emp-data-check\n",
        "import pandas as pd\n",
        "\n",
        "emp_chk = pd.read_csv(\"data/clean/emp.csv\")\n",
        "gdp_chk = pd.read_csv(\"data/clean/gdp.csv\")\n",
        "\n",
        "def both_years(df, col):\n",
        "    w = df[df[\"year\"].isin([2000, 2024])].pivot(index=\"geo\", columns=\"year\", values=col)\n",
        "    return w.dropna(subset=[2000, 2024]).index\n",
        "\n",
        "emp_both = both_years(emp_chk, \"emp_rate\")\n",
        "gdp_both = both_years(gdp_chk, \"gdp_pps\")\n",
        "flag_2024 = emp_chk[(emp_chk[\"year\"] == 2024) & emp_chk[\"geo\"].isin(emp_both)][\"emp_rate_flag\"].fillna(\"\")\n",
        "\n",
        "print(f\"Region codes: employment file {emp_chk['geo'].nunique()}, GDP file {gdp_chk['geo'].nunique()}\")\n",
        "print(f\"In the employment file but not in the GDP file: {sorted(set(emp_chk['geo']) - set(gdp_chk['geo']))}\")\n",
        "print(f\"Regions with an employment rate in 2000 and 2024: {len(emp_both)} (also with GDP in both years: {len(emp_both.intersection(gdp_both))})\")\n",
        "print(f\"2024 values flagged as break in series (b) or different definition (d): {flag_2024.str.contains('[bd]').sum()} of {len(flag_2024)}\")"
      ],
      "id": "emp-data-check",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "### Sector gross value added\n",
        "\n",
        "The sector structure comes from Eurostat table `nama_10r_3gva`: gross value added (GVA) by industry, in million euro at current prices (`CP_MEUR`), 2000-2024. Industries follow the NACE classification (`nace_r2`). The raw file holds NUTS3 regions, but it also holds Eurostat's own values for the NUTS2 regions. We added the NUTS3 values up to NUTS2 as a check (amounts can be added up; shares and rates cannot). Where all NUTS3 parts have a value, the sum is within 0.1% of the NUTS2 value in almost every case. Where some parts are missing the sum is too small, and in no case could the sum fill a NUTS2 value that Eurostat leaves empty. We therefore use Eurostat's NUTS2 values, and the cleaned file `gva.csv` contains exactly these values (@tbl-gva-nuts3)."
      ],
      "id": "28a0046d"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| label: tbl-gva-nuts3\n",
        "#| tbl-cap: 'Check of the sector GVA table: Eurostat NUTS2 values against the sum of their NUTS3 parts'\n",
        "import numpy as np\n",
        "import pandas as pd\n",
        "\n",
        "# 1. Raw Eurostat file: wide, one column per year; a value can carry a flag, for example \"1221.7 p\"\n",
        "sec_raw = pd.read_csv(\"data/raw_data/estat_nama_10r_3gva.tsv\", sep=\"\\t\", dtype=str)\n",
        "sec_raw.columns = [c.strip() for c in sec_raw.columns]\n",
        "first = sec_raw.columns[0]\n",
        "dims = first.split(\"\\\\\")[0].split(\",\")                       # freq, unit, nace_r2, geo\n",
        "sec_raw[dims] = sec_raw[first].str.split(\",\", expand=True)\n",
        "sec_raw = sec_raw[sec_raw[\"unit\"] == \"CP_MEUR\"].drop(columns=[first])    # million euro, current prices\n",
        "\n",
        "# 2. One row per region, sector and year; split each cell into its value and its flag\n",
        "sec_long = sec_raw.melt(id_vars=dims, var_name=\"year\", value_name=\"cell\")\n",
        "sec_long[\"year\"] = sec_long[\"year\"].str.strip().astype(int)\n",
        "sec_long[\"cell\"] = sec_long[\"cell\"].str.strip()\n",
        "sec_long[\"value\"] = pd.to_numeric(sec_long[\"cell\"].str.extract(r\"^([\\d.]+)\")[0], errors=\"coerce\")\n",
        "sec_long[\"flag\"] = sec_long[\"cell\"].str.extract(r\"^[\\d.]+\\s*([a-z]+)$\")[0]\n",
        "\n",
        "# 3. Our regions are the 244 EU27 NUTS2 regions of the cleaned file; we keep the six sectors and the total\n",
        "sec_regions = set(pd.read_csv(\"data/clean/gva_sector.csv\")[\"geo\"])\n",
        "six = [\"A\", \"B-E\", \"F\", \"G-J\", \"K-N\", \"O-U\"]\n",
        "sec_long = sec_long[sec_long[\"nace_r2\"].isin(six + [\"TOTAL\"])]\n",
        "official = sec_long[sec_long[\"geo\"].isin(sec_regions)].rename(columns={\"value\": \"official\"})\n",
        "nuts3 = sec_long[(sec_long[\"geo\"].str.len() == 5) & sec_long[\"geo\"].str[:4].isin(sec_regions)].copy()\n",
        "nuts3[\"nuts2\"] = nuts3[\"geo\"].str[:4]                # the NUTS2 parent is the first four characters\n",
        "\n",
        "# 4. Sum the NUTS3 values to NUTS2 (these are amounts, so summing is valid) and count the parts present\n",
        "summed = nuts3.groupby([\"nace_r2\", \"nuts2\", \"year\"]).agg(\n",
        "    sum_nuts3=(\"value\", \"sum\"), parts_present=(\"value\", \"count\"), parts_total=(\"geo\", \"nunique\")\n",
        ").reset_index().rename(columns={\"nuts2\": \"geo\"})\n",
        "chk = official[[\"nace_r2\", \"geo\", \"year\", \"official\"]].merge(summed, on=[\"nace_r2\", \"geo\", \"year\"], how=\"left\")\n",
        "chk[\"complete\"] = chk[\"parts_present\"] == chk[\"parts_total\"]       # all NUTS3 parts have a value\n",
        "chk[\"ratio\"] = chk[\"sum_nuts3\"] / chk[\"official\"]\n",
        "\n",
        "has = chk[chk[\"official\"].notna()]\n",
        "done = has[has[\"complete\"]]\n",
        "part = has[~has[\"complete\"] & (has[\"parts_total\"] > 0)]\n",
        "gone = chk[chk[\"official\"].isna()]\n",
        "check_tbl = pd.DataFrame({\"cells\": {\n",
        "    \"NUTS2 values published by Eurostat (six sectors and total)\": len(has),\n",
        "    \"... with all NUTS3 parts present\": len(done),\n",
        "    \"... of these: NUTS3 sum within 0.1% of the NUTS2 value\": int(((done[\"ratio\"] - 1).abs() <= 0.001).sum()),\n",
        "    \"... of these: largest difference (%)\": round(float((done[\"ratio\"] - 1).abs().max() * 100), 2),\n",
        "    \"... with some NUTS3 parts missing\": len(part),\n",
        "    \"... of these: no NUTS3 value present at all\": int((part[\"parts_present\"] == 0).sum()),\n",
        "    \"NUTS2 values missing at Eurostat\": len(gone),\n",
        "    \"... that the NUTS3 sum could fill (all parts present)\": int((gone[\"complete\"] & (gone[\"parts_total\"] > 0)).sum()),\n",
        "}})\n",
        "check_tbl"
      ],
      "id": "tbl-gva-nuts3",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "We keep six sectors that do not overlap: agriculture, forestry and fishing (A); industry, including energy and manufacturing (B-E); construction (F); trade, transport, accommodation and food, and information and communication (G-J); finance and insurance, real estate, professional and administrative services (K-N); and public administration, education, health, arts and other services (O-U). Codes such as C (manufacturing) or G-I are parts of these sectors, and we do not use them, because they would count the same value twice. The share of a sector is its GVA divided by the total GVA of the region, in percent. The six sectors add up to the total within 0.04%, so the shares add up to 100%."
      ],
      "id": "b672b233"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| label: gva-team-file\n",
        "# Is the cleaned file the same as the Eurostat NUTS2 values?\n",
        "team_gva = pd.read_csv(\"data/clean/gva.csv\")\n",
        "team_gva = team_gva[team_gva[\"sector\"].isin(six + [\"TOTAL\"])].rename(columns={\"sector\": \"nace_r2\"})\n",
        "both = team_gva.merge(has[[\"nace_r2\", \"geo\", \"year\", \"official\"]], on=[\"nace_r2\", \"geo\", \"year\"], how=\"outer\")\n",
        "differ = both[\"gva_meur\"].sub(both[\"official\"]).abs().gt(0.5) | (both[\"gva_meur\"].isna() != both[\"official\"].isna())\n",
        "print(f\"Cells in the cleaned file: {both['gva_meur'].notna().sum()}; Eurostat NUTS2 cells: {both['official'].notna().sum()}; cells that differ: {int(differ.sum())}\")\n",
        "\n",
        "# The six sectors add up to the total (shares are therefore close to 100%)\n",
        "tot = team_gva[team_gva[\"nace_r2\"] == \"TOTAL\"].set_index([\"geo\", \"year\"])[\"gva_meur\"]\n",
        "six_sum = team_gva[team_gva[\"nace_r2\"] != \"TOTAL\"].groupby([\"geo\", \"year\"])[\"gva_meur\"].sum()\n",
        "gap = ((six_sum / tot - 1) * 100).dropna()\n",
        "print(f\"Sum of the six sectors against the total, difference in %: min {gap.min():.3f}, median {gap.median():.3f}, max {gap.max():.3f}\")"
      ],
      "id": "gva-team-file",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
