        "### Youth unemployment \n",
        "\n",
        "Youth unemployment rates come from Eurostat table `lfst_r_lfu3rt`: the share of the labour force that is unemployed, in percent, both sexes, all education levels, for EU27 NUTS2 regions, 2000-2024. The main measure covers ages 15-24, the standard headline definition (decision 4). As a check we repeat the analysis for ages 15-29 (`Y15-29`). Both files are made from the same raw file with the same filters and the same region rules (script `clean_data.py`), so they differ only in the age group. Extra-regio territories and non-EU countries were removed, and Slovenian and Dutch regions that were only renamed were joined to their new codes. Many regions have no value in some years, and some values carry Eurostat's `u` flag for low reliability (@tbl-youth-coverage)."
      ],
      "id": "cdf1edac"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| label: tbl-youth-coverage\n",
        "#| tbl-cap: Coverage of the youth unemployment files, ages 15-24 and 15-29\n",
        "import numpy as np\n",
        "import pandas as pd\n",
        "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
        "from scipy.stats import linregress\n",
        "\n",
        "y24 = pd.read_csv(\"data/clean/youth_15_24.csv\")\n",
        "y29 = pd.read_csv(\"data/clean/youth_15_29.csv\")\n",
        "\n",
        "def wide(df, col):\n",
        "    return df.pivot(index=\"geo\", columns=\"year\", values=col)\n",
        "\n",
        "w24 = wide(y24, \"youth_unemp\")\n",
        "w29 = wide(y29, \"youth_unemp\")\n",
        "\n",
        "def coverage(df, w, label):\n",
        "    flagged = df[\"youth_unemp_flag\"].fillna(\"\").str.contains(\"u\").mean() * 100\n",
        "    return {\n",
        "        \"age group\": label,\n",
        "        \"regions (any year)\": w.shape[0],\n",
        "        \"regions with a value in 2000\": int(w[2000].notna().sum()),\n",
        "        \"regions with a value in 2024\": int(w[2024].notna().sum()),\n",
        "        \"regions with 2000 AND 2024\": w.dropna(subset=[2000, 2024]).shape[0],\n",
        "        \"regions with all 25 years\": w.dropna().shape[0],\n",
        "        \"values flagged u (%)\": round(flagged),\n",
        "    }\n",
        "\n",
        "cov = pd.DataFrame([coverage(y24, w24, \"15-24\"), coverage(y29, w29, \"15-29\")]).set_index(\"age group\").T\n",
        "cov"
      ],
      "id": "tbl-youth-coverage",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Only 123 of the 248 regions (15-24) and 151 of the 251 regions (15-29) have a value in both 2000 and 2024, therefore the catching-up test below covers about half of the regions. \n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "<!-- Other table owners: add one short paragraph for your own table here (youth unemployment, population, sector GVA). -->\n",
        "\n",
        "## Question 1: Is the gap between regions narrowing?\n",
        "\n",
        "### Measure\n",
        "\n",
        "We measure the gap with **sigma convergence**: for each year, the standard deviation of the natural logarithm of GDP per head across regions. A smaller value means regions are closer together. Using logarithms means the measure reflects percentage differences between regions, not differences in euro. We also compute the coefficient of variation (standard deviation divided by the mean), which is based on levels instead of logs.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Data and sample"
      ],
      "id": "1a4a6f17"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| label: setup\n",
        "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
        "import numpy as np\n",
        "import pandas as pd\n",
        "\n",
        "path = \"data/clean/gdp.csv\"          # columns: geo, year, gdp_pc (PPS per head)\n",
        "gdp = pd.read_csv(\"data/clean/gdp.csv\")\n",
        "gdp[\"log_gdp\"] = np.log(gdp[\"gdp_pps\"])\n",
        "\n",
        "n_years = gdp.groupby(\"geo\")[\"year\"].nunique()\n",
        "balanced = n_years[n_years == 25].index          # regions with a value in every year 2000-2024\n",
        "\n",
        "def spread(df):\n",
        "    g = df.groupby(\"year\")\n",
        "    return pd.DataFrame({\n",
        "        \"regions\": g[\"geo\"].nunique(),\n",
        "        \"sigma_log\": g[\"log_gdp\"].std(),                   # std. deviation of log GDP per head\n",
        "        \"cv\": g[\"gdp_pps\"].std() / g[\"gdp_pps\"].mean(),    # coefficient of variation\n",
        "    })\n",
        "\n",
        "A = spread(gdp)                                           # A: every region with a value that year\n",
        "B = spread(gdp[gdp[\"geo\"].isin(balanced)])                # B: only regions with all 25 years\n",
        "C = spread(gdp[~gdp[\"geo\"].str.startswith(\"RO\")])         # C: sample A without Romania"
      ],
      "id": "setup",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "The GDP file has 244 regions, but not all of them have a value in every year. @tbl-gaps lists the regions with gaps."
      ],
      "id": "ae2d7057"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| label: tbl-gaps\n",
        "#| tbl-cap: Regions without a GDP value in every year 2000-2024, by country\n",
        "span = gdp.groupby(\"geo\")[\"year\"].agg(first_year=\"min\", last_year=\"max\", years_with_data=\"count\")\n",
        "span[\"country\"] = span.index.str[:2]\n",
        "gaps = span[span[\"years_with_data\"] < 25]\n",
        "names = {\"BE\": \"Belgium\", \"FR\": \"France\", \"RO\": \"Romania\"}\n",
        "gaps_by_country = gaps.groupby(\"country\").agg(\n",
        "    regions=(\"first_year\", \"size\"),\n",
        "    first_year_with_data=(\"first_year\", \"min\"),\n",
        "    fewest_years_with_data=(\"years_with_data\", \"min\"))\n",
        "gaps_by_country.index = [names.get(c, c) for c in gaps_by_country.index]\n",
        "gaps_by_country"
      ],
      "id": "tbl-gaps",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Because of these gaps we use three samples:\n",
        "\n",
        "- **Sample A:** every region that has a value in a given year. The number of regions changes from year to year.\n",
        "- **Sample B:** only the 224 regions that have a value in every year. This sample is the same in every year.\n",
        "- **Sample C:** sample A without Romania, to isolate the effect of Romania's gap.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Results"
      ],
      "id": "91da0475"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| label: tbl-years\n",
        "#| tbl-cap: 'Number of regions and spread of log GDP per head in selected years (A: all available, C: A without Romania, B: regions with every year)'\n",
        "years = [2000, 2001, 2002, 2003, 2008, 2011, 2012, 2019, 2024]\n",
