        "### Interpretation\n",
        "\n",
        "Both samples show a fall in the spread of GDP per head (PPS) across EU regions between 2000 and 2024: by about 31% in sample A and 26% in sample B (@tbl-years, @fig-sigma). Regions therefore became more similar in income per head. The coefficient of variation also falls, but by less (about 13% in A and 10% in B).\n",
        "\n",
        "Most of the fall came before 2008. In sample B the spread fell from 0.465 to 0.393 by 2008, about 60% of the total fall of 0.121, or roughly 0.009 per year. From 2008 to 2024 it kept falling, but at about a third of that pace (roughly 0.003 per year). There was a small rise in 2020, which did not change the downward trend, and around 2012 sample B was almost unchanged.\n",
        "\n",
        "Sample A should be read with care. Eurostat has no GDP values for Romania's 8 regions in 2002-2011 (@tbl-gaps), so sample A has 232 regions in 2000, 224 in 2002 and 243 in 2012 (@fig-sigma, lower panel). Sample C (A without Romania) shows the effect: about two thirds of the drop in A between 2000 and 2002 (0.047 of 0.071) comes from the Romanian regions leaving the sample, and the rise in 2012 appears only in A. Including Romania adds 0.048 to the spread in 2000 but only 0.004 in 2024, which is why the fall looks larger in A (31%) than in B (26%).\n",
        "\n",
        "The Romanian regions started far below the rest: in 2001, 7 of the 8 were among the 12 poorest regions in the data, and their median GDP per head was about 27% of the median of all regions. By 2024 it was about 75%. Sample B is the more consistent picture, because it uses the same 224 regions in every year, but it leaves out Romania, Belgium and one French region, which have gaps. Because Romania's regions started so far below the rest and then caught up, B may understate how much narrowing took place.\n",
        "\n",
        "Overall, income per head became more similar across EU regions between 2000 and 2024, with most of the narrowing before 2008. This analysis does not show why this happened, for example whether EU funding played a role.\n",
