        "### Results and interpretation\n",
        "\n",
        "The results provide evidence of beta-convergence across EU regions between 2000 and 2024. The regression analysis shows a negative relationship between initial GDP per capita and subsequent economic growth. The regression coefficient is approximately -0.01988, with an R-squared of 0.547. The relationship is statistically significant (p < 0.001).\n",
        "\n",
        "This suggests that regions with lower initial GDP per capita generally experienced faster economic growth than regions that were initially wealthier. The comparison between the poorest and richest quarters supports this finding. The poorest quarter recorded average annual GDP growth of approximately 5.20%, compared with 2.81% for the richest quarter.\n",
        "\n",
        "The employment analysis provides additional evidence of convergence. Across the 199 regions with employment data for both 2000 and 2024, the regression coefficient is approximately -0.3433. This indicates that regions with lower initial employment rates generally experienced larger improvements in employment. The quarter of regions with the lowest initial employment rates recorded an average increase of 13.49 percentage points, compared with 6.81 percentage points for the quarter with the highest initial rates.\n",
        "\n",
        "Overall, the findings suggest that poorer EU regions experienced faster economic growth and that regions with initially lower employment rates made greater improvements in employment. However, the results do not establish that lower initial income causes faster growth. They demonstrate an association between initial regional conditions and subsequent changes.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Robustness Check: Alternative Employment Age Group (15–64)\n",
        "\n",
