        "The result holds with the wider age group. For ages 15-64 the coefficient is -0.270 (20-64: -0.343), so regions that started with lower employment still had larger increases, and the relationship is still statistically significant (p < 0.001). It is somewhat weaker: R-squared falls from 0.199 to 0.143, which means that initial employment explains about 14% of the change instead of 20%. We did not test whether the two coefficients differ significantly. The 15-64 rates are about 4.5 percentage points lower on average in 2000 (61.4% against 65.9%), which fits with many 15-19 year-olds being in education and not working, but we did not test this explanation. The two measures give almost the same change for each region (correlation 0.99), so the age group does not change which regions improved most. This check only changes the age group. It does not address the choice of regions or measurement error (see the limitations).\n",
        "\n",
        "### Limitations of this analysis\n",
        "\n",
        "- **Different regional samples for GDP and employment.**\n",
        "The GDP and employment analyses do not contain exactly the same number of regions because data availability differs between the two indicators. This limits direct comparisons between economic growth and employment improvements. The results should therefore be interpreted as separate measures of regional convergence.\n",
        "\n",
        "- **Correlation does not establish causation.**\n",
        "The regression analysis identifies a relationship between initial regional GDP per capita and subsequent economic growth. However, it does not establish that being poorer causes a region to grow faster. Other factors, such as investment, infrastructure, industrial structure, education and government policies, may also influence regional economic performance.\n",
        "\n",
        "- **Breaks in the employment series.**\n",
        "Many 2024 employment values are flagged by Eurostat as a break in the series or a different definition (see the data section). Part of the change between 2000 and 2024 may therefore come from a change in how employment was measured and not from a real change. We did not test this.\n",
        "\n",
        "- **Measurement error in the starting values.**\n",
        "Regional employment rates come from a survey, so each value has some sampling error. A region whose 2000 rate was too low by chance will tend to show a larger increase by 2024, and the reverse for a region whose rate was too high. This can make the negative coefficient look stronger than the true convergence. We did not correct for it, so the coefficients should be read as an upper estimate of how strongly low-employment regions caught up.\n",
        "\n",
        "- **Limited observation period.**\n",
        "The analysis compares the beginning and end of the 2000–2024 period. Although this provides an overview of long-term changes, it does not fully capture economic fluctuations, financial crises, the COVID-19 pandemic or other events that may have affected individual regions during the period.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Question 3: Which groups of regions behave alike?\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "### Measure\n",
        "\n",
        "We describe every region by the share of its gross value added that comes from each of the six sectors in 2000, the first year of the period. We use the start of the period so that the sector structure is not itself a result of the growth we compare it with later. Regions with a similar mix are put in the same group with k-means clustering. The shares are standardised so that every sector counts equally, each solution is the best of 20 random starts, and the random seed is fixed so that the result can be repeated. The number of groups is chosen with the silhouette score (1 means clearly separated groups, 0 means overlapping groups) and with how well the groups can be described.\n",
        "\n",
        "To separate geography from industrial structure we use the country as the measure of geography. We look at how much of the variation in each sector lies between countries, how many countries each group covers, and how well the groups agree with the countries (adjusted Rand index, ARI: 1 means identical, about 0 means no more agreement than chance). Finally we check whether the groups behave alike: their income, growth (same definition as in Question 2), change in employment rate, and the spread of income inside each group.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Data and sample\n",
