        "### Interpretation\n",
        "\n",
        "**Short answer.** Regions fall into five loose types of economy. The types are partly national, and they tell us little about growth once we know where a region lies and how rich it was at the start.\n",
        "\n",
        "**The groups.** The largest group is *industry* (88 regions, 29% of value added from industry). *Trade, transport and ICT* has 57 regions (30% from this sector, and the highest construction share), *finance and business services* has 34 (29%, and almost no agriculture), and *public services* and *agriculture* have 27 regions each (33% and 14% from their own sector). The silhouette scores are low for every number of groups (0.25 to 0.28) and do not point to one best number. Regions therefore change gradually from one type to another; the groups are a useful summary, not separate boxes. The two groups with 27 regions are small, so statements about them deserve more care.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Geography or industrial structure?** Both matter. The country explains a large part of the differences in sector mix: 62% of the variation in construction, 57% in agriculture and 55% in finance and business services, but only 28% in industry. Some groups sit in a few countries. The public services group covers 6 countries, and 23 of its 27 regions are French, German or Italian. In the agriculture group 17 of 27 regions are in Romania, Bulgaria and Greece, and 30 of the 57 trade, transport and ICT regions are Spanish or Polish. Other groups cross borders: finance and business services is spread over 17 countries and industry over 14. Overall the groups agree little with the countries (ARI 0.11). The same type of economy therefore appears on both sides of borders, but where a region lies strongly shapes which type it is. We cannot separate the two completely, because the country is only a rough stand-in for geography; our files contain no distances.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Do the groups behave alike?** They differ in growth: the agriculture group grew by 4.9% a year, trade, transport and ICT by 3.9%, finance and business services by 3.2%, industry by 3.0% and public services by 2.8% (Kruskal-Wallis p = 1e-05). But this order follows the starting income. The median GDP per head in 2000 was 6,900 PPS in the agriculture group and 27,700 in the finance group. Initial income alone explains 55% of the differences in growth between regions, and adding the group raises this to 64%. When the country is also taken into account (countries alone explain 93%), the group adds only about half a percentage point (93.2% against 92.7%; the F-test gives p = 0.003, so the effect is detectable but small). Belonging to a sector group therefore says little about growth beyond the region's country and starting level. The groups behave alike mainly because their regions started at similar income levels and lie in similar countries.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Where the pattern does not hold.** Inside the groups, the spread of income fell strongly for agriculture (0.54 to 0.21) and for trade, transport and ICT (0.43 to 0.28), less for industry (0.32 to 0.24), hardly for finance (0.28 to 0.27), and *rose* for public services (0.14 to 0.20). The regions of the public services group, mostly French, German and Italian, moved apart, so this is the group that does not follow convergence. The groups with the strongest fall contain regions from poorer and richer countries (for example Romania and Spain in the agriculture group, Poland and Austria in the trade group). The fall may therefore reflect countries catching up and not a sector effect, but we have not tested this here.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Employment.** The employment rate rose by 9.5 to 11.5 percentage points in four of the groups (trade 11.5, public services 11.2, industry 10.5, finance 9.5) and by 4.7 points in the agriculture group, which has only 17 regions with employment data. Sector group is not a strong sign of how much employment grew.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Structure itself changes.** Between 2000 and 2023 the average share of finance and business services rose by 3.1 percentage points, while agriculture fell by 1.6 and industry by 1.5 (@tbl-q3-change). 71 regions changed their finance share by more than 5 points. The groups describe the economy at the start of the period, not a fixed type."
