        "The reason is that the catching-up test only asks whether the regions that started worse improved more. It does not ask whether other regions fell behind. In 56 of the 123 regions youth unemployment (ages 15-24) was higher in 2024 than in 2000. The regions that did worst compared with the pattern are mostly Spanish and Romanian (for example ES23, ES42, RO22, RO41, RO31; ES63 is the small city of Ceuta, where numbers are noisy), while Polish, Czech and Slovak regions (PL51, PL22, CZ08, PL21, SK03) fell much more than the pattern predicts. This means that regions that started high, such as southern Italy, Poland and Slovakia, fell the most, but other regions moved the wrong way, so the overall spread did not shrink after 2008.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Which test do we believe?\n",
        "\n",
        "For the question \"is the gap closing?\" we believe the spread test more, because it measures the gap directly. The catching-up test shows who improved, but it cannot show that the gap closed. For youth unemployment we read both tests with care. The spread line jumps for data reasons (the break in 2005 and the missing German regions from 2020) and depends on the end year: in 2019 it was back at its 2000 level. The catching-up slope is probably too strong, because the starting values are noisy (about 20% of the 15-24 values are flagged as low reliability) and a region whose rate in 2000 was too high by chance looks like it improved a lot; we did not test this. We trust a result most when the tests, the two age groups and the sub-periods agree, as for GDP. For youth unemployment after 2008 the tests disagree, so we cannot say that regions became more alike.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Limitations of this analysis\n",
        "\n",
        "- Only 123 of 248 regions (ages 15-24) and 151 of 251 regions (ages 15-29) have a value in both 2000 and 2024, so the catching-up test covers about half of the regions.\n",
