        "- The set of regions changes from year to year and there are breaks in the series (2005 and 2021). From 2020 the German regions have no values, so the spread line jumps for reasons that are not about the regions.\n",
        "- The result depends on the start year and the end year (2019 against 2024).\n",
        "- The catching-up slope may be too strong because of noisy starting values (not tested).\n",
        "- We did not test whether the slopes for ages 15-24 and 15-29 differ significantly.\n",
        "- The sub-periods (2008 and 2013) are our own choice, and other cut points could give slightly different numbers.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Conclusion and limitations\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "**Answer to our question.** Yes, EU27 regions converged between 2000 and 2024, but how clearly depends on the indicator and on the period.\n",
        "\n",
        "- **Income (GDP per head, PPS).** The spread across regions fell by about 31% (all regions available each year) and 26% (the 224 regions with every year), and poorer regions grew faster: the poorest quarter grew by 5.2% a year, the richest quarter by 2.8% (slope -0.0199, R-squared 0.55). About 60% of the fall in the spread came before 2008.\n",
        "- **Employment (ages 20-64).** Regions with a lower starting rate gained more (slope -0.34, R-squared 0.20). The spread fell by 16% over the whole period, but in the crisis (2008-2013) it grew by about a third.\n",
        "- **Youth unemployment (ages 15-24).** Regions with high starting rates improved most (slope -0.50), but the spread was back at its 2000 level in 2019, so we cannot say that the gap closed.\n",
